WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Every long conversation runs into the same ceiling. A session eventually fills the token

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.560
space the model can read. For the next few minutes, we'll dig into server-side compaction,

3
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:17.360
the API's own opt-in version, not the compact command inside Cloud Code. We'll see what it does,

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.040
how it differs from your own rolling summary, and one habit that protects your facts. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:24.079 --> 00:00:28.319
A conversation isn't storage. Everything the model can see right now

6
00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:33.439
sits inside the context window, the fixed span of tokens it reads at once,

7
00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:40.159
and every message you send, every tool call, and every tool result eats into it. Compaction

8
00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:46.880
is a server-side step that kicks in once you cross a token threshold you set. By default,

9
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.279
it summarizes the whole conversation so far into a compact form, then lets the session continue

10
00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:59.439
from that summary. Durable state is the one I care about most. That's any fact you deliberately

11
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:05.440
store outside the conversation in a file or a database, so it survives no matter what happens

12
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.080
to the transcript. Everything ahead builds on that last one. So what does compaction actually do?

13
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:18.400
First, it's opt-in. You turn it on with a beta header and a context management strategy,

14
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.760
and you set the trigger threshold, which defaults to 150,000 input tokens

15
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:30.400
and can go as low as 50,000. Once it's enabled, the platform does the rest automatically. When

16
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.000
the session reaches that threshold, by default it summarizes the whole conversation up to that point

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.599
and distills it into a compact block, then continues from that summary. It doesn't keep

18
00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:47.040
your recent turns word-for-word unless you opt-in. There's a client-managed setting,

19
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.279
pause after compaction, where your own code reinserts the recent messages verbatim. But

20
00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:59.199
that's on you to wire up, not automatic. From your seat, you keep talking to the model exactly

21
00:01:59.199 --> 00:02:05.519
as before. Why does this feature exist at all? Because every long agent run collides with the

22
00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:11.360
same wall. Extended chats and multi-step agent runs fill the window fastest, so that's where

23
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.440
this earns its keep. The window is finite, old turns pile up, and eventually they crowd out room

24
00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.600
for new ones. Without some kind of help, a session just stalls once the history overflows the space

25
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.600
available. Compaction buys back room so the work can continue past that point. I want you to see

26
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.759
the intent clearly. The goal is a longer effective session with as little loss as possible of what

27
00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:41.759
actually mattered. It's not magic, and it isn't free, but it turns a hard stop into a soft one.

28
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:49.440
That alone is worth understanding as a foundation. Now the contrast that matters. Earlier in the

29
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.960
course, we taught an application-level rolling summary. That's where your own code watches how

30
00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.479
long the conversation is getting and, at some threshold, calls the model to summarize and trim

31
00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:07.199
the history itself. You own the timing, you own the prompt, you own what gets kept. Server-side

32
00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:12.960
compaction moves the heavy lifting into the platform. You still opt in and set the threshold,

33
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.720
but once it's enabled, the platform does the summarizing for you at that point. Both solve the

34
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.039
same core problem. The real question is who writes the summarize loop. With your rolling summary,

35
00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:31.119
you do. With compaction, the platform does. And you trade some control for a lot less to build.

36
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:38.559
That trade shows up as three things worth naming. First, there's less to build. Compaction runs

37
00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:45.039
inside the platform, so you skip writing, testing, and tuning your own summarize and trim loop.

38
00:03:45.039 --> 00:03:51.119
For a lot of applications, that's a genuine relief. Second, there's less control. Because the platform

39
00:03:51.119 --> 00:03:57.520
decides what the summary keeps, you give up fine-grained say over exactly which details survive,

40
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.000
so plan for some to be dropped. Third, it isn't free. The summary still costs a step to produce.

41
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.520
None of these is a flaw. They're just the shape of the deal. Knowing what you're getting is the

42
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:17.279
foundations-level point here. Here's the catch I most want to land. Any summary is lossy by nature.

43
00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:22.640
That's not a bug in compaction. It's simply what summarizing means. A fact that doesn't make it

44
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:29.040
into the summary is gone from the model's context. Exact numbers, identifiers, a snippet of code the

45
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.119
model wrote earlier, all of that can quietly drop out when the history is compressed. And once it's

46
00:04:35.119 --> 00:04:41.279
gone, the model can't recover it because the raw turns it came from are no longer there to read.

47
00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:48.000
So the lesson is blunt. Treat the summarized history as a gist, not a ledger. Never treat the

48
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.600
transcript as your only copy of something you actually need. If it matters, it has to live

49
00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.799
somewhere the summary can't erase. The fix is a habit, not a setting you flip on. As you work,

50
00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:06.880
write your durable facts to a file or a database, then re-read that store when you need them.

51
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:12.799
Instead of trusting that an old turn is still sitting in context, let compaction do what it's

52
00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:18.160
good at, which is handling the bulk of the conversation while your own store holds the

53
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.640
handful of things that truly can't be lost. Think of it as a division of labor. The platform keeps

54
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.399
the session flowing. You keep the essentials safe outside it. This pairs naturally with a tool built

55
00:05:30.399 --> 00:05:38.640
exactly for that job, the memory tool. Think of a compacted history as a gist, a summary,

56
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.559
not a ledger. The platform buys you room by summarizing what came before, and a summary

57
00:05:44.559 --> 00:05:50.959
is lossy by definition. So exact numbers and identifiers can quietly vanish. That's the trade

58
00:05:50.959 --> 00:05:56.880
you're accepting when you turn it on. It holds up as long as anything you truly can't lose

59
00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.200
already lives somewhere outside the transcript.

60
00:05:59.839 --> 00:06:02.880
That's where we'll leave this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

